\f0 If I strip away my later planning failures while 
\f1\b \strokec2 keeping the richness instead of smoothing it
\f0\b0 \strokec2 , the creative development you built was this:\
A force prepares to take Sublime Point and discovers that Shakti\'92s automated defences are frighteningly exact. Drones become expensive confetti. Soldiers can be immobilised and humiliated without being killed. The military can probably take the place, but it will hate every minute of doing so.\
Then the Elvi begin arriving for an entirely different reason: they are coming to celebrate the Holy Momma, the imminent One After the One and Christmas. They come in guitars-and-tents mode rather than assault mode, in an assortment of vehicles whose commitment to completing the journey exceeds their mechanical ability. Cars get pushed and towed onwards.\
The nearby golf course begins absorbing the overflow. Camping is tolerated so long as fairways and greens remain clear. The Elvi comply. They had no plan to play golf and brought no clubs. They borrow them. Golf infects the vigil. Tee times become serious. Performances and golf begin interfering with one another. Green fees can be settled with music in the clubhouse.\
The gathering develops the ordinary physical apparatus of people staying somewhere longer than expected. Somewhere among that wider clutter sits the CAKE, BAGEL AND ROLL van, unexplained and uncelebrated.\
The children of the Airborne patrician class prove far more welcoming than their parents. They come to visit \'93friends\'94, fill commercial accommodation, then open access to enormous private houses. Sixteen-bedroom mansions fill with friends, friends of friends, musicians and Elvi. Guitars turn up beside pools. Elvi end up in them.\
Music spreads into all the local pubs. Younger residents love it. The surrounding settlement becomes socially entangled with the Elvi. Eventually even UDF troops are turning up at performances, still in uniform, having a thoroughly good time. A sergeant sent to retrieve them can end up staying for the encore.\
Throughout all of this, three ordinary-looking guitars contain stolen Gyges discs and the narrative gives the reader no reason to pick them out.\
And the larger structural reality, which the book must make the reader discover rather than explain, is that 
